<p>Explanation: The anchor elements on the navbar make use of the <strong>::after</strong> selector to create a pseudo-element on the anchor tag, which initially has the opacity set to 0 so you can't see it. 
<br/>
<br/>
There is a pseudo-class <strong>:hover</strong> attached to the anchor element along with another <strong>::after</strong> pseudo-element which works as detecting hover events on the anchor element and adding an element after the anchor element with certain properties. This element is only added when the hover event is detected. 
<br/>
<br/>
In this case, the properties are just opacity and the transform for animation. Remember that there is already an <strong>::after</strong> pseudo-element attached to the anchor element with certain properties, so this just works as setting the opacity and transform, so that you can see the ______ floating in below the anchor element. 
<br/>
<br/>
The animation for the arrow next to the "Get Started" anchor element works in a similar way.
<br/>
Read the documentation on MDN for CSS pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements. 
<br/><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/::after" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/::after</a>
<br/><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/:hover" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/:hover</a></p>
